The '''2016 FA Cup final''' was an [[association football]] match between [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]] and [[Manchester United F.C.|Manchester United]] on 21&nbsp;May 2016 at [[Wembley Stadium]] in [[London]], England, organised by [[the Football Association]] (FA). It marked the [[List of FA Cup Finals|135th final]] of the [[Football Association Challenge Cup]] (FA Cup) and was the showpiece match of English football's primary cup competition. It was Manchester United's first FA Cup final appearance since [[2007 FA Cup final|2007]], when they lost 1–0 to [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]. Crystal Palace were playing their second FA Cup final, the previous occasion being [[1990 FA Cup final|in 1990]], when they lost to Manchester United after a [[Replay (sports)|replay]] following a 3–3 draw in the first match.

[[Mark Clattenburg]], from [[Consett]], [[County Durham]], was the [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the match, which was played in front of 88,619 spectators.  The first half was goalless although Clattenburg was the subject of some controversy when he awarded Crystal Palace a [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] instead of playing [[Glossary of association football terms#A|advantage]] after Manchester United's [[Chris Smalling]] was adjudged to have [[Fouls and misconduct (association football)|fouled]] [[Connor Wickham]]. After coming on as a second-half [[Substitute (association football)|substitute]], [[Jason Puncheon]] gave Crystal Palace the lead when he scored from close range past [[David de Gea]] in the Manchester United goal. The lead lasted three minutes before [[Juan Mata]]'s deflected [[Volley (association football)|volley]] beat [[Wayne Hennessey]], the Crystal Palace goalkeeper. Regular time ended with a scoreline of 1–1, sending the final into [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]]. Just before the interval in the additional period, Smalling was [[sent off]] after receiving a second [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]], becoming the fourth player to be dismissed in an FA Cup final. [[Jesse Lingard]], who had been brought on to replace Mata close to the end of regular time, then shot from distance, sending the ball into the top corner of the Crystal Palace goal to give Manchester United a 2–1 victory, and their twelfth FA Cup.

Manchester United's [[Wayne Rooney]] was named as [[player of the match|man of the match]]. The victory was [[Louis van Gaal]]'s only trophy as Manchester United's [[Manager (association football)|manager]] and he was sacked two days after the final, to be replaced by [[José Mourinho]]. By winning the final, Manchester United qualified for both the [[2016 FA Community Shield]] and the group stage of the [[2016–17 UEFA Europa League]].

==Background==
The [[FA Cup]] is an annual [[knockout tournament]] involving professional and amateur men's [[association football|football]] clubs in the [[English football league system]].{{sfn|O'Leary|2017|p=63}} It is the world's oldest football cup competition.<ref name=finals>{{Cite web |url=http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |publisher=[[The Football Association]] |title=FA Cup Finals, 1872 – today |access-date=9 July 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170613182709/http://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/more/finals |archive-date=13 June 2017 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="ArsenalManUtdChelseaLiverpool">{{Cite web |url=https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |work=[[FourFourTwo]] |access-date=5 October 2020 |title=Who has won the most FA Cups? The 12 most successful clubs in the world's oldest competition |first=Sam |last=Blitz |date=3 August 2020 |archive-date=5 October 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201005103153/https://www.fourfourtwo.com/features/who-has-won-most-fa-cups-12-most-successful-clubs-arsenal-man-utd-chelsea-liverpool |url-status=live }}</ref> The 2016 final was the 135th final to be played since it was first held in 1872.<ref name=finals/>

In the two league matches between the sides during the regular season, the fixture at [[Selhurst Park]] in London in October 2015 ended in a goalless draw while the game at [[Old Trafford]] the following April ended in a 2–0 victory to Manchester United.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-united/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Crystal%20Palace/ | website = 11v11 | publisher = AFS Enterprises | access-date = 11 May 2021 | title = Manchester United football club: record v Crystal Palace | archive-date = 7 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210507170127/https://www.11v11.com/teams/manchester-united/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Crystal%20Palace/ | url-status = live }}</ref> Crystal Palace's top scorer during the regular season was [[Dwight Gayle]] who had scored 7 goals in 11 appearances in all competitions, while three players – [[Yannick Bolasie]], [[Yohan Cabaye]] and [[Connor Wickham]] – had 6 each.<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=646&teamTabs=stats&season_id=145 | website = Soccerbase | publisher = Centurycomm | access-date = 11 May 2021 | title = Crystal Palace squad details – 2015/16 – regular season | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203715/https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=646&teamTabs=stats&season_id=145 | url-status = live }}</ref>   [[Anthony Martial]] was Manchester United's leading scorer, with 17 goals, including 2 in the FA Cup, followed by [[Wayne Rooney]] on 15 (also 2 in the FA Cup).<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1724&teamTabs=stats&season_id=145 | website = Soccerbase | publisher = Centurycomm | access-date = 11 May 2021 | title = Man Utd squad details – 2015/16 – regular season | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203721/https://www.soccerbase.com/teams/team.sd?team_id=1724&teamTabs=stats&season_id=145 | url-status = live }}</ref>  Manchester United went into the final with a record of 11 wins from 18 FA Cup finals, one behind [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] in both FA Cup wins and FA Cup Final appearances.<ref name=rsssf /> United last played a final [[2007 FA Cup final|in 2007]], the first at the new Wembley, where they lost 1–0 after extra time to [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]]. Their last title victory was [[2004 FA Cup final|in 2004]], a 3–0 win against [[Millwall F.C.|Millwall]] at [[Cardiff]]'s [[Millennium Stadium]].<ref name=rsssf>{{cite web|title=England FA Challenge Cup Finals |first = James M. | last = Ross |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablese/engcuphist.html|url-status=live|access-date=23 April 2016  |website=[[RSSSF]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160512091912/http://www.rsssf.com/tablese/engcuphist.html |archive-date=12 May 2016}}</ref><ref name=thefa>{{Cite web | url = https://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/stories/fa-cup-finals | publisher = [[The Football Association]] | title = FA Cup Finals – the winner's list | access-date = 26 April 2021 | archive-date = 10 April 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210410202646/https://www.thefa.com/competitions/thefacup/stories/fa-cup-finals | url-status = live }}</ref> Crystal Palace's only previous FA Cup final was the [[1990 FA Cup final|1990 final]], which they lost to Manchester United after a replay when the first match had ended in a 3–3 draw.<ref name=thefa/>

