As a [[Premier League]] team, Crystal Palace started their campaign in the third round. There, they were drawn against fellow Premier League team [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] away at [[St Mary's Stadium]] on 9 January 2016.  Crystal Palace took the lead when [[Joel Ward (footballer)|Joel Ward]] scored in the 29th minute from a [[Jason Puncheon]] pass.  Six minutes after half time, [[Oriol Romeu]] equalised for Southampton following [[Cuco Martina]]'s [[Save (goaltender)|saved]] shot.  Midway through the second half, [[Wilfried Zaha]] restored Crystal Palace's lead, scoring with a [[Volley (association football)|volley]] after the Southampton goalkeeper [[Maarten Stekelenburg]] had kept out Puncheon's shot.  No further goals were scored and the match ended 2–1 to Crystal Palace.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35211521|title=Southampton 1–2 Crystal Palace|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=11 May 2021|date=9 January 2016|archive-date=20 October 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181020152139/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35211521|url-status=live}}</ref>  In the fourth round, Crystal Palace hosted another [[Glossary of association football terms#T|top-flight]] team in [[Stoke City F.C.|Stoke City]] at Selhurst Park on 30 January.  Zaha scored in the first half after beating the Stoke City defence, striking the ball past [[Jakob Haugaard]] for the only goal of the game.  The visiting side had made eight changes to their team from their previous league match, prompting their manager [[Mark Hughes]] to say "We were a little bit stretched, we had a number of players unavailable."<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35389007|title=Crystal Palace 1–0 Stoke City|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=11 May 2021|first=Michael|last=Emons|date=30 January 2016|archive-date=27 January 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210127071521/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35389007|url-status=live}}</ref>

In the fifth round, Crystal Palace were drawn against their third consecutive Premier League opposition, meeting [[Tottenham Hotspur F.C.|Tottenham Hotspur]] on 21 February away at [[White Hart Lane]].  Although Tottenham Hotspur had a number of chances to score, including two shots from [[Harry Kane]] which were saved by [[Wayne Hennessey]] and [[Dele Alli]] striking both goalposts with a shot, Crystal Palace secured a 1–0 victory.  [[Martin Kelly (footballer)|Martin Kelly]] scored his first goal since 2011 in first-half [[stoppage time]] from close range after receiving the ball from Zaha.  The win meant Crystal Palace advanced to the quarter-finals of the FA Cup for the first time since the [[1994–95 FA Cup|1994–95 competition]].<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35572137|title=Tottenham Hotspur 0–1 Crystal Palace|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=11 May 2021|first=Saj|last=Chowdhury|date=21 February 2016|archive-date=8 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108101129/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35572137|url-status=live}}</ref>  There, they had their first game against a lower-division team, facing [[EFL Championship|Championship]] side [[Reading F.C.|Reading]] at the [[Madejski Stadium]] on 11 March.  Although Crystal Palace dominated possession, the first half ended goalless.  With five minutes of the game remaining, Reading defender [[Jake Cooper (footballer, born 1995)|Jake Cooper]] was [[sent off]] after receiving a second [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]], fouling Bolasie and conceding a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] which was scored by Cabaye. [[Fraizer Campbell]] secured the win with a goal from close range four minutes into stoppage time.  Crystal Palace won 2–0 and progressed to the semi-final for the first time in 21 years.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35723265|title=Reading 0–2 Crystal Palace|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|access-date=11 May 2021|date=11 March 2016|first=Tom|last=Rostance|archive-date=8 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108142357/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35723265|url-status=live}}</ref>

On 24 April, Crystal Palace faced [[Watford F.C.|Watford]] at [[Wembley Stadium]], a [[Glossary of association football terms#N|neutral venue]], in a repeat of the [[2013 Football League Championship play-off final]].  Bolasie gave Crystal Palace an early lead when he headed the ball into the Watford goal after [[Damien Delaney]] flicked on a [[Corner kick|corner]] from Cabaye. Watford's [[Étienne Capoue]] was stretchered off the pitch in the 30th minute following a tangle with Bolasie.  Ten minutes into the second half, [[Troy Deeney]] equalised for Watford when he scored with a header from [[José Manuel Jurado]]'s corner.  Six minutes later, Crystal Palace retook the lead when Wickham converted [[Pape Souaré]]'s cross to make it 2–1 which remained the final score.<ref>{{cite web|last=Johnston|first=Neil|title=Crystal Palace 2–1 Watford|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/36065460|access-date=11 May 2021|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|date=24 April 2016|archive-date=8 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108094838/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/36065460|url-status=live}}</ref>

