As a Premier League team, Manchester United also entered 2015–16 FA Cup in the third round, hosting [[Sheffield United F.C.|Sheffield United]] of [[EFL League One|League One]] at Old Trafford on 9 January 2016.  [[Substitute (association football)|Substitute]] [[Memphis Depay]] was fouled in second-half stoppage time by [[Dean Hammond]] for a penalty kick, from which Rooney scored the only goal of the match. Manager [[Louis van Gaal]] was under pressure for Manchester United's poor form prior to the match.<ref>{{cite web|last=Rose|first=Gary|title=Manchester United 1–0 Sheffield United|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35211626|access-date=11 May 2021|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|date=9 January 2016|archive-date=8 November 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201108104737/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35211626|url-status=live}}</ref>  In the fourth round, Manchester United travelled to [[Pride Park Stadium|Pride Park]] to play [[Derby County F.C.|Derby County]] who were in a [[2016 Football League play-offs|play-off place]] in the Championship. Rooney scored the first goal in the 16th minute from outside the [[penalty area]], but [[George Thorne (footballer)|George Thorne]] equalised eight minutes before half time.  Midway though the second half, [[Daley Blind]] restored Manchester United's lead, scoring with a low strike from [[Jesse Lingard]]'s cross.  [[Juan Mata]]'s 83rd-minute goal after a run from Martial secured a 3–1 victory for Manchester United and relieved Van Gaal of further pressure; it was the first time in 15 games that the team won by a margin of more than one goal.<ref>{{cite web|last=Magowan|first=Alistair|title=Derby County 1–3 Manchester United|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35378959|access-date=11 May 2021|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|date=29 January 2016|archive-date=22 February 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210222074957/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35378959|url-status=live}}</ref>

[[File:West Ham v Manchester United April 2016 FA Cup.jpeg|200px|thumb|right|alt=West Ham United and Manchester United players|West Ham United and Manchester United players  entering the field for their FA Cup replay]]
On 22 February, Manchester United played the fifth round against [[Shrewsbury Town F.C.|Shrewsbury Town]], who were in 21st position in League One, away at [[New Meadow]]. [[Chris Smalling]] opened the scoring for Manchester United with his first goal in the FA Cup eight minutes before half-time.  Mata doubled their advantage in first-half stoppage time with a direct [[Free kick (association football)|free kick]] before Lingard confirmed a 3–0 win just after the hour mark, converting [[Ander Herrera]]'s pass.  Manchester United were forced to play with only ten players for the final fourteen minutes of the match after [[Will Keane]] came off injured and no substitutes remained.<ref>{{cite web|last=McNulty|first=Phil|authorlink=Phil McNulty|title=Shrewsbury Town 0–3 Manchester United|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35576165|access-date=23 April 2016|publisher=[[BBC Sport]]|date=22 February 2016|archive-date=23 March 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160323111455/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35576165|url-status=live}}</ref>  Manchester United hosted their sixth-round match against top-flight opponents [[West Ham United F.C.|West Ham United]] on 13 March. The visitors took the lead midway through the second half after [[Dimitri Payet]]'s {{convert|30|yd|adj=on}} free kick beat [[David de Gea]] in the Manchester United goal.  With seven minutes remaining, the hosts equalised when Martial scored from a cross from Herrera, ending the match 1–1. Due to the draw, a [[Replay (sports)|replay]] was required.<ref>{{cite web |last=McNulty |first=Phil |authorlink=Phil McNulty |title=Manchester United 1–1 West Ham United |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35738603 |access-date=11 May 2021 |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=13 March 2016 |archive-date=26 February 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210226102658/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35738603 |url-status=live }}</ref> This took place the following month at the [[Boleyn Ground]], the final FA Cup match at the ground.  After a goalless first half, [[Marcus Rashford]] gave Manchester United the lead in the 54th minute when he curled a shot past [[Darren Randolph]], the West Ham United goalkeeper, into the top corner of the net. [[Marouane Fellaini]] made it 2–0 midway through the second half, scoring from Martial's cross. West Ham halved the deficit in the 79th minute when [[James Tomkins (footballer)|James Tomkins]] scored with a header.  Although West Ham dominated the closing stages, including having a goal by [[Cheikhou Kouyaté]] disallowed, the match ended 2–1 to Manchester United.<ref>{{cite web| last=McNulty| first=Phil| authorlink=Phil McNulty| title=West Ham United 1–2 Manchester United| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/35974944| access-date=11 May 2021| publisher=[[BBC Sport]]| date=13 April 2016| archive-date=14 January 2021| archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210114153337/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/35974944| url-status=live}}</ref>

On 23 April, Manchester United faced [[Everton F.C.|Everton]] at Wembley, a neutral venue, in the semi-finals.   Fellaini gave Manchester United the lead in the 34th minute when he scored from close range against his former team. After the interval, [[Timothy Fosu-Mensah]] fouled Everton's [[Ross Barkley]], but de Gea saved [[Romelu Lukaku]]'s subsequent penalty kick. With 15 minutes to go, Everton substitute [[Gerard Deulofeu]] sent in a cross which was deflected into Manchester United's goal off Smalling for an [[own goal]] which levelled the score. Three minutes into stoppage time, Herrera set up Martial who struck the ball past the Everton goalkeeper [[Joel Robles]] to secure a 2–1 win for Manchester United and progression to a record-breaking 19th FA Cup final.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/36060627 | title = Everton 1–2 Manchester United | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 11 May 2021 | last = McNulty | first = Phil | authorlink = Phil McNulty | date = 23 April 2016 | archive-date = 8 November 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201108132403/https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/36060627 | url-status = live }}</ref>

A bomb scare at Old Trafford on the final day of the Premier League on 15 May meant that Manchester United's final league game, against [[AFC Bournemouth|Bournemouth]], was postponed and rearranged for 17 May.  This left Manchester United with four days to prepare for the final, two days fewer than Crystal Palace.<ref>{{cite web|title=Manchester United v Bournemouth rescheduled for Tuesday, live on Sky Sports|url=http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11661/10283952/manchester-united-v-bournemouth-rescheduled-for-tuesday-may-17|access-date=1 February 2017|publisher=[[Sky Sports]]|date=16 May 2016|archive-date=15 March 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170315030144/http://www.skysports.com/football/news/11661/10283952/manchester-united-v-bournemouth-rescheduled-for-tuesday-may-17|url-status=live}}</ref>

==Match==
===Pre-match===
The [[Referee (association football)|referee]] for the final was [[Mark Clattenburg]] from [[Consett]], [[County Durham]], who was [[Assistant referee (association football)|assisted]] by [[John Brooks (referee)|John Brooks]] and Andrew Halliday.  The [[fourth official]] was [[Neil Swarbrick]] while [[Michael Salisbury]] acted as the [[reserve assistant referee]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/apr/28/mark-clattenburg-fa-cup-final-crystal-palace-manchester-united-referee | work = [[The Guardian]] | access-date = 11 May 2021 | date = 28 April 2016 | title = Mark Clattenburg to referee Crystal Palace v Manchester United in FA Cup final | archive-date = 8 November 2020 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20201108101400/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2016/apr/28/mark-clattenburg-fa-cup-final-crystal-palace-manchester-united-referee | url-status = live }}</ref>  The match was broadcast live in the United Kingdom by both the [[BBC]], providing free-to-air coverage on [[Match of the Day]], and [[BT Sport]] acting as the pay TV alternative.<ref name="FA Final details">{{Cite web | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20180213203611/https://www.thefa.com/news/2016/apr/29/2016-emirates-fa-cup-final-kick-off-time  | url = https://www.thefa.com/news/2016/apr/29/2016-emirates-fa-cup-final-kick-off-time | access-date = 12 May 2021 | date = 29 April 2016 |  archive-date = 13 February 2018 | url-status = dead | publisher = [[The Football Association]] | title = Kick-off time confirmed for the 2016 Emirates FA Cup Final}}</ref>  Each club received an allocation of 28,780 tickets. This was an increase on previous seasons from 71% of available seats for supporters of the two opposing teams to 80%. All ticket prices were also reduced by £5 each.<ref name="FA Final details"/>  An inaugural tournament was held on 7 May in which a fan of each of the 64 teams who reached the third round competed in a knock-out football video game tournament in rooms around Wembley Stadium. The prize was tickets to the actual final.<ref>{{cite news|title=Gamers named for inaugural Emirates FA Cup tournament|url=http://www.thefa.com/news/the-fa-cup/2016/apr/gamers-named-for-final-250416|publisher=[[The Football Association]]|date=25 April 2016|access-date=11 May 2021|archive-date=13 May 2021|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203725/https://www.thefa.com/news/2016/apr/25/gamers-named-for-final-250416|url-status=live}}</ref>  The financial prize for winning the FA Cup Final was £1.8{{nbsp}}million while the runners-up  would receive £900,000.<ref name="FA Final details"/>  Manchester United were considered clear favourites to win the final, both in the media and by bookmakers.<ref name=guardasit4/><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2641388-crystal-palace-vs-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-odds-live-stream-prediction | publisher = [[Bleacher Report]] | title = Crystal Palace vs. Manchester United: FA Cup Final Odds, Live Stream, Prediction | first = Tom | last = Sunderland | access-date = 11 May 2021 | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203721/https://bleacherreport.com/articles/2641388 | url-status = live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web | url = https://betting.betfair.com/football/fa-cup/fa-cup-final-betting-crystal-palace-fans-view-170516-556.html | publisher = [[Betfair]] | access-date = 11 May 2021 | title = The Palace view on the FA Cup Final: Zaha can inspire Eagles to success | first = Ed | last = Malyon | date = 17 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203725/https://betting.betfair.com/football/fa-cup/fa-cup-final-betting-crystal-palace-fans-view-170516-556.html | url-status = live }}</ref>

Ahead of [[Kick-off (association football)|kick-off]], [[Tinie Tempah]] performed alongside [[Lewisham and Greenwich NHS Trust#Choir|The Lewisham and Greenwich NHS Trust Choir]] before the sides were presented to [[Prince William, Duke of Cambridge]].<ref>{{Cite web | url = https://www.thefa.com/news/2016/may/18/tinie-tempah-to-perform-at-the-emirates-fa-cup-final | publisher = [[The Football Association]] | title = Tinie Tempah to perform at Emirates FA Cup Final | date = 19 May 2016 | access-date = 14 May 2021 | archive-date = 14 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210514124403/https://www.thefa.com/news/2016/may/18/tinie-tempah-to-perform-at-the-emirates-fa-cup-final | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=bbc14>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=14 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 12 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (14 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203738/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/14 | url-status = live }}</ref> The [[God Save the Queen|national anthem]] was sung by [[Karen Harding]].<ref name=guardasit4>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live?page=with:block-57404756e4b0e361e755c744#liveblog-navigation | work = [[The Guardian]] | date = 21 May 2016 | access-date = 11 May 2021 | title = Crystal Palace 1–2 Manchester United (aet): FA Cup final – as it happened! (4 of 4) | first = Alan | last = Smith | archive-date = 5 February 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210205154857/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live?page=with:block-57404756e4b0e361e755c744#liveblog-navigation | url-status = live }}</ref>  Van Gaal made two changes to his starting line-up from the side that competed in his side's previous match, the last league game against Bournemouth: [[Marcos Rojo]] and Fellaini came in for [[Cameron Borthwick-Jackson]] and Lingard, the latter dropping to the substitutes' bench.<ref name=bbc17>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=17 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 12 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (17 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203721/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/17 | url-status = live }}</ref>  Crystal Palace's manager [[Alan Pardew]] recalled six players to his starting eleven: Zaha, Hennessey, [[Scott Dann]], Bolasie, Cabaye and Wickham returned while Puncheon and Gayle were listed as substitutes.<ref name=bbc17/>  Manchester United lined up in a [[Formation (association football)|4–1–4–1 formation]] with [[Michael Carrick]] playing as a defensive midfielder and Rashford the sole striker.  Their opposition adopted a [[Formation (association football)#4–2–3–1|4–2–3–1 formation]], with Wickham playing up front on his own.<ref name=bbc17/>

===Summary===
====First half====
[[File:Mark Clattenburg 2016.jpg|upright|thumb|alt=Mark Clattenburg|Mark Clattenburg was the match referee.]]
After a slight delay resulting from the overrunning pre-match entertainment, Crystal Palace kicked off around 5:30{{nbsp}}p.m. on 21 May 2016 in front of 88,619 spectators.<ref name=guardasit4/><ref name=bbc14/>  In the third minute, Crystal Palace won the first corner of the match but Cabaye's [[Set piece (football)|set piece]] was easily caught by de Gea.  Manchester United won their first corner four minutes later, and Mata's eventual cross was cleared by [[Mile Jedinak]] ahead of Fellaini.   This was followed by a series of corners for Manchester United, but none could be converted, culminating in Fellaini heading wide from Blind's pass in the tenth minute.  Rooney then struck a shot from distance; the ball took a deflection off Dann but was gathered by Hennessey at the second attempt after he initially fumbled it.<ref name=guardasit4/>  In the 17th minute, Crystal Palace counter-attacked and Wickham was brought down by Smalling – Wickham got up and ran on with the ball and scored past de Gea but Clattenburg had declined to play the [[Glossary of association football terms#A|advantage]], disallowed the goal and showed Smalling the first yellow card of the game.  De Gea was forced to tip the ball over the crossbar from the resulting free kick from Cabaye.<ref name=guardasit3>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live?page=with:block-5740a010e4b0eebe5b627b7e#liveblog-navigation | work = [[The Guardian]] | date = 21 May 2016 | access-date = 11 May 2021 | title = Crystal Palace 1–2 Manchester United (aet): FA Cup final – as it happened! (3 of 4) | first = Alan | last = Smith | archive-date = 18 September 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170918021231/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live?page=with:block-5740a010e4b0eebe5b627b7e#liveblog-navigation | url-status = live }}</ref>

In the 22nd minute, Mata received the ball from Fellaini and his low shot from the edge of the Crystal Palace penalty area was saved by Hennessey.  Three minutes later, Fellaini's header took a deflection off Jedinak and passed just wide of the Crystal Palace goal.  Bolasie's long-range shot was saved by de Gea before Wickham struck the ball wide of Manchester United's goal.  Zaha then played in a cross which Wickham was unable to reach.  With twelve minutes of the first half remaining, Rashford made a run down the right wing and crossed the ball to Martial whose first-time shot was cleared by a diving Ward.  Within two minutes, Zaha won the ball from Blind but was tackled by Rooney, and Clattenburg denied Crystal Palace's appeal for a penalty.<ref name=bbc12>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=12 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 12 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (12 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203748/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/12 | url-status = live }}</ref>  In the 38th minute, Carrick's shot from {{convert|25|yd}} went high over the Crystal Palace bar and two minutes later, Rojo fouled Ward and became the second Manchester United player to be booked.  Mata was then shown the yellow card for a high and late tackle on Souaré before Clattenburg brought the half to a close.<ref name=guardasit3/><ref name=bbc11>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=11 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 12 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (11 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203750/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/11 | url-status = live }}</ref>

====Second half====
Neither side made any changes to their personnel during the interval and Manchester United kicked off the second half.  Within two minutes, Dann fouled Rashford and became the first Crystal Palace player to be booked.  In the 52nd minute, Rashford flicked the ball to Fellaini whose shot from inside the Crystal Palace penalty area struck the crossbar with Hennessey stationary in the goal.<ref name=guardasit3/><ref name=bbc10>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=10 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (10 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203723/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/10 | url-status = live }}</ref>  Two minutes later, Cabaye's free kick from the right side of the pitch deflected off Smalling's head and found Jedinak whose shot went high over the Manchester United goal.<ref name=guardasit3/><ref name=bbc9>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=9 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (9 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203724/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/9 | url-status = live }}</ref>  Martial's 61st-minute header from an [[Antonio Valencia]] cross struck the Crystal Palace goalpost before Delaney was booked for a foul on Rojo.  The Manchester United player could not continue and was replaced by [[Matteo Darmian]] in the 66th minute.<ref name=bbc9/>  Rashford then went down with a knee injury after being involved in a clash with Zaha: unable to play on, he was substituted for [[Ashley Young]], while Crystal Palace made their first change of the afternoon with Puncheon coming on for Cabaye.<ref name=bbc8>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=8 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (8 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203735/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/8 | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=guardasit2>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live?page=with:block-5740a84de4b0e361e755c7ef#liveblog-navigation | work = [[The Guardian]] | date = 21 May 2016 | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Crystal Palace 1–2 Manchester United (aet): FA Cup final – as it happened! (2 of 4) | first = Alan | last = Smith | archive-date = 12 February 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210212220557/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live?page=with%3Ablock-5740a84de4b0e361e755c7ef#liveblog-navigation | url-status = live }}</ref>

In the 77th minute, Crystal Palace won a corner which was taken by Puncheon.  The ball was headed clear by Fellaini and fell to Ward whose high cross-field pass found Puncheon on the left-hand side of the Manchester United penalty area.  He controlled the ball before striking it with his left foot past de Gea to give Crystal Palace the lead.  Manchester United equalised three minutes later through Mata.  Rooney made a {{convert|30|yd|adj=on}} run before sending in a cross which Fellaini passed to Mata.  His volley took a deflection off Ward and passed Hennessey low into the Crystal Palace goal to level the score at 1–1.<ref name=bbc8/><ref name=guardasit2/>  With six minutes of regular time remaining, Crystal Palace made their second substitution with Wickham being replaced by Gayle, before Rooney was shown the yellow card for a late tackle on Ward.  In the 87th minute, Souaré's cross was volleyed goalbound by Zaha but the ball went out for a corner.  Mata was then replaced by Lingard as the game headed into five minutes of stoppage time, during which Crystal Palace made their final change of the match with the injured Dann being replaced by [[Adrian Mariappa]].  Zaha's late shot hit the side netting of the Manchester United goal and regular time ended with the scores level, sending the match into [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]].<ref name=guardasit2/><ref name=bbc7>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=7 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (7 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203723/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/7 | url-status = live }}</ref>

====Extra time====
[[File:Cskamu 31.jpg|right|upright|thumb|alt=Jesse Lingard|Jesse Lingard ''(pictured in 2015)'' scored the winning goal.]]
One minute into the first half of the additional period, Zaha went down while being challenged by Blind but Clattenburg declined the Crystal Palace appeals for a penalty.  [[Chris Waddle]], summarising for the BBC, described Crystal Palace's tactics as having become "like a training ground exercise" while Alan Smith in ''[[The Guardian]]'' conjectured that both sides had settled to play out the rest of extra time and resolve the match in a [[penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shootout]].<ref name=guardasit1>{{Cite web | url = https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live | work = [[The Guardian]] | date = 21 May 2016 | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Crystal Palace 1–2 Manchester United (aet): FA Cup final – as it happened! (1 of 4) | first = Alan | last = Smith | archive-date = 1 March 2017 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20170301055742/https://www.theguardian.com/football/live/2016/may/21/crystal-palace-manchester-united-fa-cup-final-live | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=bbc6>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=6 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (6 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203742/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/6 | url-status = live }}</ref>  In the 97th minute, Rooney's shot from distance was off-target before Hennessey punched away a corner from Blind.<ref name=bbc6/>  Fellaini was then booked for elbowing Jedinak before de Gea was forced to save Bolasie's volley from a Blind clearance.  Just before the extra time interval, Smalling was shown a second yellow card for his foul on Bolasie, becoming the fourth player in the history of the FA Cup to be sent off in the final.<ref name=guardasit1/><ref name=bbc5>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=5 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (5 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203726/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/5 | url-status = live }}</ref>

Early in the second period, Zaha passed to Gayle who struggled to cleanly strike the ball which was cleared by de Gea.  From the subsequent corner, Manchester United counter-attacked but Lingard was fouled by [[James McArthur]] who became the third Crystal Palace player to be booked.  Carrick then headed Lingard's cross wide from {{convert|8|yd|0}}.<ref name=guardasit1/><ref name=bbc4/> In the 110th minute, Manchester United took the lead:  a low cross from Valencia was partly cleared by Delaney but Lingard struck the ball from around {{convert|18|yd}} into the top corner of the Crystal Palace goal to make it 2–1.  He was booked in the aftermath for removing his shirt during the celebrations.  With eight minutes remaining, Jedinak struck the ball from distance but his shot went wide of Manchester United's goal.  Crystal Palace increased the pressure and de Gea had to save at the feet of Zaha to maintain his side's lead.  After two minutes of stoppage time, and despite attempts to score from Zaha and Bolasie, the match ended in a 2–1 victory for Manchester United who secured their first FA Cup final win since 2004.<ref name=guardasit1/><ref name=bbc4>{{Cite web | url = https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/live/football/36163462?page=4 | publisher = [[BBC Sport]] | access-date = 13 May 2021 | title = Mourinho latest & FA Cup final reaction (4 of 28) | first = Tom | last = Rostance | date = 21 May 2016 | archive-date = 13 May 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210513203747/https://www.bbc.com/sport/live/football/36163462/page/4 | url-status = live }}</ref>

